content.thinking:
Confirmed: the whole rich text markup is escaped (`&lt;p&gt;` etc.). So `@Model.BodyText` (IHtmlEncodedString) is being HTML-encoded.

Why? The Razor runtime's `Write(object)` checks `if (value is IHtmlEncodedString e) `? Actually in MVC's RazorPage `Write(object)`:
```csharp
public virtual void Write(object value)
{
    if (value is null || value == HtmlString.Empty) return;
    var content = value as IHtmlContent;
    if (content != null) { WriteTo(content); return; }
    Write(value.ToString());
}
```
`IHtmlEncodedString : IHtmlContent`? If yes → WriteTo, raw. If IHtmlEncodedString does NOT inherit IHtmlContent → `value.ToString()` then Encoder.Encode → escape.

So evidently in Umbraco 18, `IHtmlEncodedString` might NOT implement IHtmlContent! Let me check the interface. If it implements `IEncodedString` only, Razor encodes it. That would explain exactly.

Hmm wait, but earlier AGENTS content... Actually the standard Umbraco pattern for RTE in model is `@Model.BodyText` and it works in normal Umbraco views. But in Umbraco 18 maybe they changed to need `.ToHtmlString()` or `Html.Raw`.

Let me decompile `IHtmlEncodedString` from Umbraco.Cms.Core. Let me check.

